#CXRLE Pos=-6,2 Gen=9x = 7, y = 4, rule = My-Byl4.2B$.C.BDAB$2CAD2CB$3BD2B!@RULE My-BylByl's self-reproducing loop of twelve cells.Source: J. Byl, "Self-reproduction in small cellular automata", Physica D 34(1989) 295-299: Table II, the transition function for Fig. 3. As in the paper,each transition is CTRBL->I (center, top, right, bottom and left, then thecenter's next state), here the six digits C N E S W C'; rotations are notlisted (the rotate4 symmetry), and a group's default "C----", which appliesto any other neighborhood of that center, is the line with variables at theend of the group.Table II prints 56 entries, its defaults among them, where the paper's textcounts 57. With them alone, the run follows Fig. 3 until generation 22 andthen differs in one cell; the transition 31523 -> 1 makes every generation ofFig. 3 come out, so it is added, marked, in its place among the 3s.Fig. 3 is drawn as the mirror image (left and right swapped) of what Table IIgives under the paper's reading of the neighbors. The starter pattern(Byl-Loop.rle) is therefore Fig. 3's loop mirrored, and its run is Fig. 3's,mirrored: at generation 25 an exact copy of the loop of generation 0 standsbeside its parent.Re-authored for heaton-life from the paper (spec/ruletable.md).@TABLEn_states:6neighborhood:vonNeumannsymmetries:rotate4var a={0,1,2,3,4,5}var b={a}var c={a}var d={a}# C N E S W C'0000310001220001310001520002550003150003230004220,a,b,c,d,01000001000101000331000401003301004311032131125311245331,a,b,c,d,42000002001552002202020202021552023532025252,a,b,c,d,2300010300030300110300121301211301231311221311231312151312231312331312355314321314525# The one transition Fig. 3 needs that Table II doesn't print (spec/ruletable.md):3152313,a,b,c,d,34000354004344021244023244024244025204032554,a,b,c,d,35002255003255021245022205032205,a,b,c,d,2@NAMES0 quiescent1 data path2 outer wall3 extend4 turn left5 disconnect and copy